

1. High-level architecture

Modules:

MediaEngine — loads and plays video/frames

DataChannel — loads time-aligned metadata (fields, organisms, etc.)

OverlayEngine — draws visual layers on top of the media

SyncController — keeps media time and data time in lockstep

2. Media engine

Responsibility: decode and play the cinematic.

Inputs: file path or URL (local or GitHub raw)

Core functions:

loadMedia(source)

play() / pause() / stop()

seek(time)

onTimeUpdate(callback) — fires with current playback time

This is the “video player” heart.

3. Data channel

Responsibility: provide simulation data aligned with time.

Inputs: JSON/CSV/HDF5/etc. with per-frame or per-time-step data

Core functions:

loadData(source)

getStateAt(time) → { Θ, Ξ, organisms, metric, … }

This is how the viewer knows what the simulation is doing at each moment.

4. Overlay engine

Responsibility: render visual intelligence on top of the media.

Inputs: current frame + current data state

Core functions:

registerLayer(layer)

render(time, dataState, canvasContext)

Example layers:

time-field heatmap

meta-model regime map

organism outlines

metric grid

labels / HUD (Δτ, model name, etc.)

Each layer implements:

interface OverlayLayer {
    id: string
    render(time, dataState, ctx): void
}

5. Sync controller

Responsibility: glue everything together.

Subscribes to MediaEngine.onTimeUpdate

For each tick:

t = currentTime

state = DataChannel.getStateAt(t)

OverlayEngine.render(t, state, ctx)

Core loop:

onMediaTimeUpdate(t):
    state = dataChannel.getStateAt(t)
    overlayEngine.render(t, state, overlayCanvas)

6. Minimal flow (end-to-end)

MediaEngine.loadMedia(video.mp4)

DataChannel.loadData(simulation.json)

User hits Play

MediaEngine emits time updates

SyncController pulls data for that time

OverlayEngine draws layers on top of the video

